{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-47708",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let $A$ represents the event described in the question. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =\\frac{n}{N},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N$ is the total number of ways to pick $3$ marbles out of $10,$ and $n$ is the number of ways to choose exactly $1$ blue and $2$ red marbles.\n\nSince the order in which he picks the marbles does not matter, we use combinations.\n\nFirst, we compute $N.$ The number of ways to pick $3$ marbles from a total of $10$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N={}_{10}{C}_{3} & =\\frac{10!}{7!\\cdot 3!}=120.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $n.$ The computation of $n$ can be divided into two steps: \n\n- the number of ways to choose $1$ (out of $6$) blue marble is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{6}{C}_{1}=\\frac{6!}{5!\\cdot 1!}=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the number of ways to choose $2$ (out of $4$) red marbles is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{4}{C}_{2} & =\\frac{4!}{2!\\cdot 2!}=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the results from the above steps, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n={}_{6}{C}_{1}\\cdot {}_{4}{C}_{2}=6\\cdot 6=36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =\\frac{36}{120}=\\frac{3}{10}=0.3=30%.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
